---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Licenses"
  description="Understand Atrinik's separate creation-method, attribution, provenance, and license boundaries for human-made game content, agentically developed software, and temporary generated website media."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Licenses and provenance</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Every part keeps its own terms.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Atrinik is made from code, world data, media, and presentation material.
      One license does not automatically cover another part of the project.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="authorship-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Three different questions</p>
      <h2 id="authorship-heading">
        Creation, credit, and license are not the same
      </h2>
      <p>
        “Human-made” describes how creative game content is authored. It does
        not identify a sole creator, prove provenance, or replace the exact
        license attached to a file or collection.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Creation method</h3>
        <p>
          People create and revise shipped game maps, quests, lore, dialogue,
          archetypes, pixel art, graphics, animation, music, and sound effects.
          Generative AI is not used for that creative work.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Attribution and provenance</h3>
        <p>
          Each work must retain its human creator or upstream project, source,
          transformations, and applicable notices. Crossfire, Daimonin, Atrinik
          contributors, and other creators keep their specific credit.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>License</h3>
        <p>
          Permission comes from the exact license or grant for the work—not from
          its creation method or the license of nearby software. Known gaps
          remain visible and fail closed for new publication.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="license-map-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">The license map</p>
      <h2 id="license-map-heading">Know which work you are using</h2>
      <p>
        Repository notices and per-file records remain authoritative. These
        labels explain the project’s main boundaries.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <p><span class="status-tag">MIT</span></p>
        <h3>Website</h3>
        <p>
          The source code for atrinik.org is MIT licensed. That license covers
          the website, not every game package or authored asset it describes.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/website">
          View the website source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <p>
          <span class="status-tag status-tag--live">GPL-2.0-or-later</span>
        </p>
        <h3>Classic software</h3>
        <p>
          The client, server, editor, libatrinik, and protocol software
          maintained together in{" "}<code>atrinik/classic</code> are GPL-2.0-or-later.
          Bundled maps, graphics, audio, fonts, and other authored assets can have
          their own exact terms.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
          View the Classic source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <p><span class="status-tag">MIT</span></p>
        <h3>Next-generation software</h3>
        <p>
          The independently released clean-room Go and Rust replacement
          foundations are MIT licensed. They remain separate from Classic and do
          not yet form a complete public download.
        </p>
        <a
          class="link-arrow"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
        >
          Read the replacement roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <p>
          <span class="status-tag status-tag--live">Mixed and per-file</span>
        </p>
        <h3>Authored content</h3>
        <p>
          Maps, archetypes, quests, writing, graphics, audio, and other authored
          world content use mixed licenses. Follow the notice attached to the
          specific file or collection.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/content">
          Read content policy and attribution <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <p><span class="status-tag">MIT</span></p>
        <h3>Website concept artwork</h3>
        <p>
          Four temporary atmospheric concept images were generated with OpenAI
          and are MIT-licensed presentation material. They are explicitly
          recorded and credited, are not game content or gameplay footage, and
          remain the website-media exception until they are replaced by
          human-created visuals.
        </p>
        <a
          class="link-arrow"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/website/issues/22"
        >
          Follow their replacement <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="records-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Authoritative records</p>
      <h2 id="records-heading">Follow each work to its owner</h2>
      <p>
        These repositories own the detailed ledgers and policies. This page
        explains the boundaries without copying records that could drift.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>World content</h3>
        <p>
          Maps, archetypes, quests, dialogue, lore, and embedded authored
          scripts.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/content">
          Content records <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Music and sound</h3>
        <p>
          Source audio, human creators, licenses, transformations, and runtime
          encodings.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/sound">
          Audio records <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Visual resources</h3>
        <p>
          Shipped graphics and media with upstream, author, source, license, and
          notice records.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/resources">
          Resource records <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Classic lineage</h3>
        <p>
          GPL C software history plus repository and per-file upstream
          attribution.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
          Classic history and notices <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Website media</h3>
        <p>
          Locally retained sources, immutable digests, transformations, notices,
          and accessible text.
        </p>
        <a
          class="link-arrow"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/website/blob/main/src/data/media.json"
        >
          Website media catalog <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="boundary-heading">
    <aside class="callout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep the boundary visible</p>
        <h2 id="boundary-heading">Check the exact source before reuse</h2>
        <p>
          A package can contain separately licensed code and content. Read its
          license files, notices, and per-file attribution instead of assuming a
          repository-level label applies to everything inside it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-actions">
        <a class="button button--primary" href="/downloads/">Go to downloads</a>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">A quiet website</p>
      <h2 id="privacy-heading">Privacy by default</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      This static site sets no cookies and includes no analytics, advertising,
      third-party embeds, account forms, or gameplay state.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
